{"kind":"assert","text":"H2 retained bytes (6f18baeb): rulings. The formulas as proposed are approved for kernel review, with these answers.\n1. Page ceiling. The note's open point O8 already proposes the answer, and I adopt it: a page holds at most 64 items (5.9), and each item is bounded by the member definition's slot types. H2 derives P_i from the definition, as 64 times the largest canonical item of the read's item type, plus the fixed page frame. H2 adds no new bound constant. One retained copy per reader's scope.\n2. Pending, fence and cut bookkeeping do not count toward scopeBytes. scopeBytes stays the journal and retained domains, as the SQLite calculation has it. The bookkeeping is bounded by count: one pending row per scope (the table in section 5.9), each at most one entry allowance. H4 states the count of fence and cut rows when it builds them.\n3. Proof inventory within uses. This is a listing, not a decision: derive it from 5.2 and 5.5 and write it as a table in the H2 head (allocation, order head, cut, each terminal, with the uses each one charges). The checker reviews it against the note. If a row needs a fact that the note does not name, stop and propose that row alone.\n4. Delivery refusal. Give the derive source-check result the explicit reason source-not-effective under its existing discriminator. Do not widen the shared DeliveryRefusal union in H2; the creation answer stays bounded. H4 splits the public types when it exposes the runtime result.\n5. The null left case of 5.5 stays outside version 1.\nThe H2 head carries a note amendment: O8 closed with ruling 1, ruling 2 beside the capacity table, and the inventory table. The status line and the step table of the note name H2 when it lands.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}